## Original Design

```markdown
---
title: "Configuration Distribution System"
category: "Foundational Infrastructure"
difficulty: "Hard"
tags: ["config", "control-plane", "rollouts", "validation", "versioning", "fleet-management"]
---

## Overview

This system is a high-availability configuration control plane + distribution path for a large fleet. It stores configurations as immutable, versioned artifacts; validates them against schemas; and distributes updates via a **push-notify / pull-fetch** model so the fleet can update quickly without turning the control plane into a broadcast bottleneck.

The key insight is to treat “a config change” as a **rollout with guardrails**, not a write to a key-value store. The system separates (1) **publishing** a version (upload + validate + sign), from (2) **activating** it (progressively shifting cohorts), and it makes rollback a metadata flip rather than a data rewrite. This keeps the system boring and operable: Postgres for truth, object storage for blobs, and a lightweight pub/sub for invalidation.

## What Makes This Hard

Naive designs optimize for “read the latest value” and ignore that the real blast radius comes from *activation*. The trap: schema validation is necessary but not sufficient—many failures are semantic (timeouts, feature interactions, latent assumptions). If “latest” auto-propagates to every node, a single bad publish becomes a fleet-wide incident.

The second trap is the update storm. If 100k agents all pull immediately after a change, you either (a) DDoS your own control plane, or (b) slow-roll unintentionally. The elegant solution is to make push a **hint**, use pull with jitter, and serve artifacts through caching layers.

## Requirements

### Functional Requirements
- Publish config bundles with schema validation and immutability (audit-friendly, reproducible).
- Support both pull (agents polling) and push (near-real-time notification) without requiring long-lived connections from every node.
- Versioning with explicit activation targets (by service, environment, region, cohort).
- Safe rollouts: canary, progressive rollout, and fast rollback.
- Strong authentication/authorization and tamper resistance (prevent config injection).

### Scale Targets
- Fleet: 200k nodes across 20 regions.
- Config size: median 10 KB, p99 200 KB (bundled per service/env).
- Change rate: 1,000 publishes/day; 50 “hot” rollouts/day that must converge in <5 minutes.
- Read path: steady-state polling at 60s with jitter → ~3,300 rps globally; bursty convergence after notifications.
- Availability: 99.95% for reads; 99.9% for publishes (reads are the lifeblood, writes can queue briefly).

## Key Design Decisions

- **Push-notify / pull-fetch**
  - Chose: pub/sub sends “new desired version for cohort X” notifications; agents pull artifacts over HTTPS.
  - Rejected: server-initiated push streaming configs to every node.
  - Why: avoids fanout bottlenecks, tolerates intermittent connectivity, and makes load predictable with jitter/backoff.

- **Immutable artifacts + metadata activation**
  - Chose: configs stored as content-addressed blobs (hash) with signed manifests; Postgres stores rollout state (“cohort → version”).
  - Rejected: mutable key-value updates as “the config.”
  - Why: rollback becomes an O(1) pointer change; audit is trivial; agents can safely cache and verify.

- **Agent-managed last-known-good (LKG)**
  - Chose: a local config agent that atomically swaps versions, runs validation hooks, and can self-revert.
  - Rejected: every application implementing its own config logic.
  - Why: the hard part is safe activation; centralizing this in an agent prevents a hundred subtly-broken clients.

## Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A["Config CLI/UI"] --> B["Config API"]
  B --> C["Postgres (metadata)"]
  B --> D["Blob Store (artifacts)"]
  B --> E["Validator/Signer"]
  B --> F["Pub/Sub (notify)"]
  G["CDN/Edge Cache"] --> D
  H["Config Agent"] --> G
  H --> B
  F --> H
```

### Components

- `Config CLI/UI`: Creates bundles, selects schemas, and starts rollouts; must make “who gets this when” explicit.
- `Config API`: The control plane. Owns authz, writes metadata, issues pre-signed artifact URLs, and exposes “desired version for my cohort.”
- `Postgres (metadata)`: Source of truth for schemas, versions, rollout state, cohort definitions, and audit logs. Strong consistency matters for activation.
- `Blob Store (artifacts)`: Stores immutable config bundles and manifests. Cheap, durable, horizontally scalable.
- `Validator/Signer`: Enforces schema validation and produces signed manifests. Separates “this is well-formed” from “this is activated.”
- `Pub/Sub (notify)`: Carries invalidations (“rollout updated”) to agents. Not a delivery pipe; losing messages is survivable because agents still poll.
- `CDN/Edge Cache`: Serves artifacts at scale and absorbs post-notify bursts; prevents control-plane overload.
- `Config Agent`: Runs on every node. Polls desired version, subscribes to notifications, downloads artifacts, verifies signatures, applies atomically, and keeps LKG.

## Deep Dive: Safe Rollouts and Rollbacks (The Hardest Part)

The system models rollout as a **desired-state pointer** per cohort, not “latest config.” Each config publish creates an immutable artifact `artifact_hash` and a semantic `version_id` (monotonic per service/env). Activation is a separate transaction that updates `(cohort, desired_version_id)` in Postgres. This cleanly separates *publishing* from *blast radius*.

