Rollouts progress via cohorts: e.g., `canary-1%`, `canary-10%`, `region-us-east`, `global`. Progression is driven by explicit operator action or an automated controller that gates on health signals (SLO error budget, crash loops, config-agent self-revert rate). The key is that gating uses **independent telemetry** (service metrics), not just “agent applied successfully,” because semantic failures often manifest only under real traffic.

Rollback is a metadata flip: set the cohort’s desired version back to the prior known-good `version_id`. Agents converge quickly (notify + pull), and even without control-plane coordination an individual agent can self-revert to `lkg/` if its acceptance hook or post-apply health probe fails. This dual-path rollback (central + local) is what keeps config incidents from becoming outages.

## Trade-offs

| Optimized For | Sacrificed |
|--------------|------------|
| Fast, safe rollback | “Always latest” simplicity |
| Predictable read scaling | True push delivery semantics |
| Auditability and reproducibility | Slightly higher publish complexity |
| Fleet resilience (LKG + caching) | More logic in the agent |

