---
title: "Configuration Distribution System"
category: "Foundational Infrastructure"
difficulty: "Hard"
tags: ["config", "control-plane", "rollouts", "validation", "versioning", "fleet-management"]
---

## Overview

This system is a high-availability configuration control plane + distribution path for a large fleet. It stores configurations as immutable, versioned artifacts; validates them against schemas; and distributes updates via a **push-notify / pull-fetch** model so the fleet can update quickly without turning the control plane into a broadcast bottleneck.

The key insight is to treat “a config change” as a **rollout with guardrails**, not a write to a key-value store. The system separates (1) **publishing** a version (upload + validate + sign), from (2) **activating** it (progressively shifting cohorts), and it makes rollback a metadata flip rather than a data rewrite. This keeps the system boring and operable: Postgres for truth, object storage for blobs, and a lightweight pub/sub for invalidation.

## What Makes This Hard

Naive designs optimize for “read the latest value” and ignore that the real blast radius comes from *activation*. The trap: schema validation is necessary but not sufficient—many failures are semantic (timeouts, feature interactions, latent assumptions). If “latest” auto-propagates to every node, a single bad publish becomes a fleet-wide incident.

The second trap is the update storm. If 100k agents all pull immediately after a change, you either (a) DDoS your own control plane, or (b) slow-roll unintentionally. The elegant solution is to make push a **hint**, use pull with jitter, and serve artifacts through caching layers.

